## PART I — THE BRAIN THAT WAS NOT READY

### Chapter 1 — The Savanna Brain in the Cloud

Human cognitive architecture evolved over approximately 300,000 years to manage social groups of 50–150 individuals (Dunbar, 1992), process information arriving at biologically manageable rates, and make decisions in environments where **most variables were visible and local**. The internet has subjected this same architecture, in the span of 30 years, to the social complexity of **eight billion simultaneous actors**, a planetary information stream of effectively infinite volume, and a decision environment where the consequences of actions are invisible, global, and delayed.

According to evolutionary psychology, human cognition was shaped in specific environments. In order to adapt to these varying environments, humans developed technologies such as tools, language, and writing — and these in turn further affected human cognition. Today, the Internet also shapes our cognition. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5993299/)

The recent advent and widespread use of digital technologies — whether the Internet and social media a few decades ago, or AI and large language models such as ChatGPT more recently — have particularly impacted human cognition and its neurobiology, and are about to deeply reshape brain functioning in ways we can predict and in ways we cannot yet conceive. [Frontiers](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/human-dynamics/articles/10.3389/fhumd.2024.1475438/full)

The thesis of this chapter: the brain's cognitive limits are not defects. They are the expected outputs of an optimization process that had no reason to anticipate the Cloud.

---

### Chapter 2 — The Death of Information Scarcity

For the entirety of recorded history, the dominant epistemic problem of civilization was **not knowing enough**. Libraries, universities, newspapers, and encyclopedias were solutions to **scarcity**. The internet eliminated scarcity and replaced it with its structural opposite: **infinite abundance**. This inversion is underappreciated. The cognitive filters humans built — trust in authority, deference to institutions, reliance on narrative coherence — were calibrated to navigate scarcity. They are systematically maladaptive in conditions of abundance.

Attentional overload occurs when the demands of the environment exceed the capacity of an individual's attentional resources. The digital world presents a wide range of stimuli — alerts, personalized notifications, social media updates, emails, tweets, calendar reminders, texts, and news feeds — all of which compete for an individual's attention. The abundance of information can create a sense of urgency to stay connected, leading to continuous partial attention and multitasking. [Frontiers](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cognition/articles/10.3389/fcogn.2023.1203077/full)

---

### Chapter 3 — When Attention Becomes the Supreme Resource

In the attention economy, limited time and cognitive resources, along with the human brain's inability to make decisions in parallel, make attention a scarce resource — one that becomes increasingly valuable as the Internet and social media systems provide more channels and information items from which an information consumer must choose. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.1185)

This chapter argues that the commodification of attention is the **original sin** of the digital economy: by transforming the direction of human consciousness into a tradeable asset, the attention economy created a structural incentive to hijack cognition rather than serve it. Social media platforms are not, in this framework, aberrations. They are rational actors in a system whose logic is cognitively predatory.

