## PART II — THE INTERNET HAS CHANGED THE SPECIES

### Chapter 4 — The Global Village Is Not a Village

Marshall McLuhan's 1962 concept of the "global village" was visionary but structurally incomplete. A village functions not because people are connected, but because **connection generates mutual accountability, shared context, and legible consequence**. The internet connects without generating any of these. The result is a simulacrum of community that produces the emotional activation of tribal belonging — in-group solidarity, out-group hostility — without the epistemic grounding that real community provides.

---

### Chapter 5 — The War of Realities

When AI can seamlessly simulate anything, people lose trust in any information — even genuine facts — and reality itself splinters into fragmented belief-based versions. [Medium](https://medium.com/epistemic-security-studies/epistemic-collapse-in-the-age-of-ai-generated-hyperreality-79fc179497df)

This chapter analyzes the phenomenon of **epistemic fragmentation**: two individuals with equivalent intelligence, education, and good faith can inhabit entirely different information universes and reach mutually incompatible conclusions about factual reality. This is not a failure of rationality. It is the **expected output of personalization algorithms** whose optimization function is engagement, not truth. The "war of realities" is less a culture war than an information-architecture war.

---

### Chapter 6 — The Outrage Economy

The digital economy has discovered an asymmetry with profound civilizational consequences: **negative affect drives more engagement than positive affect**. Anger, fear, moral indignation, and contempt generate more clicks, shares, comments, and time-on-platform than satisfaction, curiosity, or joy. The rational corporate response to this discovery is the systematic optimization of content for outrage production. The civilizational consequence is the **metabolization of conflict as infrastructure**.

Digital overload is exhausting our emotional and cognitive resources, diminishing our potential. [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org/stories/2025/08/obsession-innovation-humanity-deficit/)

---

### Chapter 7 — Algorithms That Know Us Better Than We Know Ourselves

This chapter addresses the emergence of **behavioral prediction systems** — algorithms capable of modeling individual psychological profiles with sufficient precision to anticipate and shape decisions before they are consciously formulated. Drawing on Kosinski (2024) and related literature, this chapter argues that the combination of mass data collection and machine learning has produced a **structural asymmetry of self-knowledge**: platforms know more about a user's cognitive and emotional patterns than the user does.

AI's role in behavioral prediction and content manipulation raises concerns about cognitive autonomy and decision-making biases. [RSIS International](https://rsisinternational.org/journals/ijriss/articles/a-systematic-review-of-the-impact-of-artificial-intelligence-digital-technology-and-social-media-on-cognitive-functions/)

